    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The only remaining \"sequence\" reference is the doc comment explicitly noting they were dropped. The implementation is complete and green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned owner identity so the `customerCode` is now `<REGION>-<HASH8>`, with every derived value flowing from that new identity. `BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests pass (the acceptance test's `coreCustomerCodeIsRegionHash` now sees codes like `NSW-16E6609E`).\n\nChanges:\n\n- **`AssignCustomerCode.java`** \u2014 replaced the old `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` per-city sequence logic with `<REGION>-<HASH8>`. REGION comes from `OwnerRegion.fromPostcode(postcode)` (falling back to `UNKNOWN`), HASH8 from the new hash primitive. The `OwnerRepository` dependency (only needed for the sequence count) is gone.\n\n- **`OwnerIdentity.java`** \u2014 added `customerHash(telephone, lastName)`, the first 8 upper-case hex chars of SHA-256 over `normalizedTelephone + lastName` (telephone canonicalized to E.164), reusing the existing `sha256Hex` primitive.\n\n- **`OwnerRegion.java`** \u2014 removed the postcode-then-city `locality(...)` derivation and added `regionOf(customerCode)`, which reads the REGION component straight off the assigned code (`UNKNOWN` when absent). This is the behavioural change: locality now tracks the code's region (postcode-derived) rather than the old city fallback.\n\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `locality(owner)` now delegates to `OwnerRegion.regionOf(owner.getCustomerCode())`; refreshed the membership-number doc example. `membershipNumber`, `checkDigit`, and the `AuditOwnerCreated` log all read `customerCode`, so they automatically reflect the new identity with no logic change.\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `customerCode`, `membershipNumber`, and `locality` descriptions/examples to the new format.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: the spec says REGION is \"derived from the postcode,\" but owners can be created without a postcode (the base test helpers do this). I fall back to `UNKNOWN` in that case, matching the existing `UNKNOWN` locality convention and keeping the code a valid `[A-Z0-9]+` token.",
